

**Numerical Methods**

**Example 1.** To evaluate \(J_{n}(1.55)\), \(n\)=0, 1, 2, \(\cdot\), each to 5 decimals. The recurrence relation

\[J_{n-1}(x)+J_{n+1}(x)=(2n/x)J_{n}(x)\]

can be used to compute \(J_{0}(x)\), \(J_{1}(x)\), \(J_{2}(x)\), \(\cdot\) \(\cdot\), successively provided that \(n\)\(<\)\(x\), otherwise severe accumulation of rounding errors will occur. Since, however, \(J_{n}(x)\) is a decreasing function of \(n\) when \(n\)\(>\)\(x\), recurrence can always be carried out in the direction of decreasing \(n\).

Inspection of Table 9.2 shows that \(J_{n}(1.55)\) vanishes to 5 decimals when \(n\)\(>\)\(7\). Taking arbitrary values zero for \(J_{0}\) and unity for \(J_{0}\), we compute by recurrence the entries in the second column of the following table, rounding off to the nearest integer at each step.

